Z||000000||JohnnyK||05-13-2001||11:13 PM||||Well on the way to my Wal-mart (shaddup) interview, I had an urge to get out in the parking lot and check the hood/trunk (I dunno why). It's a good thing I did, because my battery hold down strap (I modified the tray because I don't have all the original pieces, os it used a rubber strap over the battery) snapped the battery was in the pulley. It leaked all over the pavement, but my car still started fine. It's still damn heavy. Would any of you guys risk filling it with water and then sealing it up again?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||voyagerspe||05-13-2001||11:32 PM||||They used to make a product called "battery renew" or something like that.  Its basically battery acid.  Go to a good auto parts store you should be able to buy some acid to top off your battery.  If you do use water buy some distilled water dont use tap water or spring water.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Indiana_resto_guy||05-14-2001||12:22 AM||||BUY A NEW BATTERY!<BR>If you rubbed a hole in the side, SORRY CHARLIE!, you won't be able to fix it!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Nashco||05-14-2001||01:28 AM||||Not true, you can fix one, because I have.  I HIGHLY recommend against it though, as it was just a temporary fix.  Go buy a new battery...hell...you're Walmart material, it's not like you can't afford it!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  Sorry, I had to do it.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
